# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): the activities and sources below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://archive.org/details/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The deck was compiled by an AI agent working for Anton Wiklund, and checked by AI agents and by machines (scripts checking the count, the ids, the overlap with Basic Korean, NFC and the uniqueness of every back in every language; the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators and a second SHACL validator); no person has reviewed the cards."@en ,
                 "Selection: 150 phrases in nine groups, after the deck's proposal (issue #122): greetings and goodbyes (18), introducing yourself (13), not understanding (13), questions (14), at a restaurant or café (22), shopping (12), getting around (17), needs and help (16), and short answers and set expressions (25). The list is the authoring agent's own: the phrases a beginner needs most in those situations, leaving out every phrase of Basic Korean (안녕하세요, 감사합니다, 미안합니다, 실례합니다, 괜찮습니다, 여보세요, 네, 아니요 and the others; 네? (\"pardon?\") and 아니요, 괜찮아요 (\"no, thank you\") are new uses of two of them), and keeping one phrase for each meaning, and no back the same as one of Basic Korean's."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The front is the phrase in Hangul only, in today's standard spelling and spacing, in the everyday polite style (-요); set expressions that are normally formal (처음 뵙겠습니다, 잘 먹겠습니다, 죄송합니다, 수고하셨습니다 and others) keep their usual form, and their notes give the other forms. Questions end in a question mark, other phrases have no final punctuation, and … marks a word the learner fills in. The back gives one natural equivalent in each of English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French, unique in each language, with du, tu and tú among peers and Sie, usted and vous where the Korean is honorific or said to staff and strangers. A note, the same in all five languages, gives where it helps the more formal or casual form, the usual reply, or when the phrase is used."@en ,
                 "Card ids are the letter-by-letter Revised Romanization of the Hangul, syllable by syllable, without hyphens, sound changes or punctuation (처음 뵙겠습니다 cheoeumboepgetseupnida, 이거 얼마예요? igeoeolmayeyo), the rule of the library's Korean decks; they are addresses, not shown as text."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the deck is CC0 1.0. Its text is the authors' own, written with the help of AI; the two sources it was checked against for content, the FSI course (a work of the United States Government) and Wikidata (CC0 1.0), allow any reuse, and English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0) was used to verify the cards only, with no text taken from it."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources (AI and machine)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 150 cards against the three sources, each searched by a separate AI agent with scripts: the FSI course's OCR text and page images, Wikidata through SPARQL and the Wikibase API, and English Wiktionary's definitions. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No source contradicts a card. The FSI course has 48 of the phrases, 36 of them in another speech level, and the words of 59 more; Wikidata has a lexeme for one phrase (네) and lexemes or items for the words of 88 more cards; Wiktionary supports 142 cards and partly supports 8. Its one point on spelling, that 한번 for \"once\" is spelled 한 번, does not apply to 다시 한번 (\"once more\"), in which the National Institute of Korean Language writes 한번 as one word. The FSI course gives 똑바로 가십시오 for \"go straight ahead\"; the card says 쭉 가세요, which the Korean review found more natural, and names 똑바로 가세요 in its note; the counts here are for the cards as published, with 쭉 가세요, which the course does not have."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Korean (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 150 cards line by line: the Korean of each front and the Korean quoted in the notes, for correctness, naturalness, politeness level, standard spelling and spacing, and the notes' claims about usage. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors; spelling and spacing were found standard on every card. 10 warnings, all applied: 똑바로 가세요 became 쭉 가세요; 죄송합니다 is now glossed as respectful rather than \"very sorry\", its note saying it is more respectful, not more formal, than 미안합니다; the notes of six formal set expressions now give their -요 forms; and a note no longer spaced the copula from the noun. 6 of 7 suggestions were applied as notes (한국어로 뭐라고 해요?, 제가 살게요, 두 명이요, 여기요, and when 잘했어요 and 먼저 가세요 are said); a new card for 괜찮아요? was not added."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "English and Swedish (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 150 cards' English and Swedish backs and notes, for natural everyday equivalents, accuracy, uniqueness, American English spelling and consistent notes. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 1 error, applied: \"to take away\" became \"to go\". 8 warnings, applied but one: \"the check\", \"January 1\", \"how do I do it?\", idiomatic Swedish for taking food away, getting home safely and the two emergency calls; for 잘 먹겠습니다 the Swedish back is \"tack, det ser gott ut (före måltiden)\" rather than the reviewer's wording. Most suggestions were applied (\"store\" for \"shop\", more idiomatic Swedish backs and notes)."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "German (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 150 cards' German backs and notes, for natural equivalents, consistent forms of address, uniqueness and notes that match the English. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors. 11 warnings, all applied: Sie in seven requests to strangers and staff, Minimarkt for a Korean convenience store, \"was hast du gesagt?\" for 뭐라고요?, and two wordings. The 8 suggestions were applied."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 150 cards' Spanish and French backs and notes, for natural equivalents, consistent forms of address and gender marking, punctuation, uniqueness and notes that match the English. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 7 errors, all applied: the speaker's gender marked (o/a, (e)) on seven backs. 13 warnings, all applied: usted and vous in the requests to strangers, \"¿qué fecha es hoy?\", 수고하세요 in Spanish, the Korean in a French note, the 119 note without a French service name and a gendered note. All 22 suggestions were applied."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final review (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The built release, after the rounds above were applied, read again from six angles by six AI agents working independently of the compiling agent (Korean; English and Swedish; German; Spanish and French; the record and licensing; the repository's rules), each finding then put to two further AI agents told to refute it. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 38 findings: 2 errors and 19 warnings, each put to two further agents told to refute it, and 17 suggestions. Both errors were in the record: it had named English Wiktionary as a source the deck was derived from, though no text was taken from it, and it counted the replaced card 똑바로 가세요 in the FSI figures; both were corrected, and the FSI source now has the same title and creator as in Basic Korean and quotes the evidence for its status. On the cards: 잘 가요 had Swedish, Spanish and French backs the same as Basic Korean's 안녕히 가세요 and now says hej hej, chao and salut; 직진하세요 is no longer said to be for drivers; and address, gender and wording were made consistent in nine backs and notes. Two warnings, that German, Spanish and French should ask 영어 할 줄 알아요? formally and that German should say frohes Schaffen for 수고하세요, were refuted by both verifiers and not applied; the note on 몇 살이에요? now names elderly people in English and French only, as the verifiers advised. Most suggestions were applied."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole release: scripts checking the count, the ids against the library's romanization rule, the overlap with Basic Korean (no front and no back the same), NFC and the uniqueness of every back in every language; the library checker (npm run library) with the app's shapes, DCAT-AP and SKOS; and a second SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck)."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms: the scripts found 150 cards with unique ids, no front or back the same as Basic Korean's, every back unique in each language and every text in NFC; the library checker found the library valid with this deck in it, and pySHACL found all 233 documents it checks conform."@en .

<https://archive.org/details/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText>
    dcterms:title "Korean Basic Course, Volume 1 (Foreign Service Institute, 1968), as scanned on the Internet Archive" ;
    dcterms:creator "B. Nam Park; edited by Augustus A. Koski; Foreign Service Institute, U.S. Department of State" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Checking: the Internet Archive's OCR text of Volume 1 (romanized Korean and English), searched for every card, with three page images read for the Hangul; Volume 2 in the same item, whose OCR lost its Hangul, for its English only. Of the 150 cards, 12 are in Volume 1 as they stand, 36 in another speech level (mostly its formal -(스)ㅂ니다 forms), 59 only by their words and 43 not at all, among them expressions newer than the course (와이파이, 카드, 편의점, 파이팅). Where the course's Korean of 1968 differs from today's (처음 뵙습니다, 약방, 가까와요, 아이구), the cards follow today's standard. A work of the United States Government: the title page reads \"B. Nam Park … Foreign Service Institute, Washington, D.C. 1968\" and \"For sale by the Superintendent of Documents, U.S. Government Printing Office\". 17 U.S.C. § 105: \"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government\". Section 105 applies in the United States; what the deck takes from the course is checking of standard phrases and their meanings, not its text."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata (Korean lexemes and items)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors (Wikimedia Foundation)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Checking: Korean lexemes and items queried for every card's phrase and its words on 16 October 2026. Wikidata has few Korean lexemes: one phrase (네) has a lexeme, whose only sense is \"yes\", 88 more cards have lexemes or items for their words (건배 and 파이팅 among the items, 파이팅 its Korean label with 화이팅 an alias) and 61 have none. https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://en.wiktionary.org/>
    dcterms:title "English Wiktionary (Korean entries)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wiktionary contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Verification only; no text was taken from it. Every card's phrase, or its words in their dictionary forms, was looked up on 16 October 2026: 18 phrases have an entry of their own and the words of the other 132 have entries, two verbs only through their nouns (포장 for 포장하다, 부탁 and 부탁하다 for 부탁드리다). The definitions support 142 cards and partly support 8, where the card gives the phrase's use and Wiktionary only its literal sense (네? as \"pardon?\", 보고 싶어요 as \"I miss you\", 수고하세요 on leaving); none contradicts a card. Text is available under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wiktionary:Copyrights)."@en .

<#cheoeumboepgetseupnida>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
